Azure Backup and disaster recovery solve related but different problems.

Backup primarily focuses on preserving data and creating recovery points that can be used to restore information after deletion, corruption, or other data-loss events.

Disaster recovery focuses on restoring the availability of workloads after a major infrastructure or site failure.

Key differences include:

| Aspect            | Backup                    | Disaster Recovery                                   |
| ----------------- | ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Primary purpose   | Protect data              | Restore service availability                        |
| Typical scenario  | Deleted or corrupted data | Datacenter or region failure                        |
| Recovery focus    | Data restoration          | Workload recovery                                   |
| Key consideration | Retention                 | RTO and RPO                                         |
| Example           | Restore a deleted file    | Recover an application after infrastructure failure |

For example, a company may use backups to recover accidentally deleted records while using disaster recovery mechanisms to restore its application after a major datacenter outage.

A real-life use case is an enterprise combining both approaches to protect against everyday data loss as well as large-scale disasters.
